          "title": "Treatment",
          "text": "• Empirical therapy is a third-generation cephalosporin (ceftriaxone or cefotaxime), started immediately.\n• Penicillin G is an option once susceptibility is confirmed.\n• Adjunctive dexamethasone is given with suspected bacterial meningitis per guidelines.\n• Intensive care for shock, with fluids and vasopressors.\n• Droplet precautions for the first 24 hours of effective therapy."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prophylaxis and Vaccines",
          "text": "• Close contacts receive a single dose of ciprofloxacin, or rifampin, or ceftriaxone (choice depends on age and pregnancy).\n• Quadrivalent conjugate vaccine (MenACWY) for adolescents, asplenia, complement deficiency, and pilgrims to the Hajj.\n• Serogroup B vaccines (4CMenB, MenB-FHbp) for at-risk groups and outbreaks."
        },
        {
          "title": "Resistance",
          "text": "• Reduced penicillin susceptibility occurs in some regions.\n• Ciprofloxacin-resistant isolates have been reported, so check local patterns.\n• Ceftriaxone resistance is very rare."
        }
